{"term":"avoir des relations","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To have sexual relations; a neutral euphemism for intercourse, most commonly encountered in the expanded form 'avoir des relations sexuelles' in medical, legal, and journalistic registers.","english_term":"to have relations","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The expansion 'avoir des relations sexuelles' removes all ambiguity; the shortened form is context-dependent. Among the most clinically neutral options available to non-specialists."}
{"term":"passer à l'acte","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_complete","concept":"intercourse_complete","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To go ahead and do it; a broad euphemism for crossing from flirtation, foreplay, or intention into actual sexual intercourse.","english_term":"to go all the way","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also used outside sexual contexts to mean acting on any intention (a plan, a threat); the sexual reading requires tonal or situational cues."}
{"term":"la chose","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_activity_generic","concept":"sexual_activity_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'The thing'; a maximally vague deictic euphemism for sex or the sex act, used to avoid naming the activity directly.","english_term":"the thing","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Deliberately non-specific; tends to index older, more reticent speakers or formal social contexts where naming the act directly would be considered indecorous."}
{"term":"les galipettes","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_activity_general","concept":"sexual_activity_general","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Somersaults' or 'tumbles'; playful slang for sexual romping or lovemaking, typically in the phrase 'faire des galipettes', evoking acrobatic frolicking in bed.","english_term":"to romp","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is gymnastic somersaults or cartwheels. The sexual sense is affectionate and light-hearted; registers as slightly old-fashioned but not archaic."}
{"term":"les ébats","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_activity_general","concept":"sexual_activity_general","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Frolics' or 'gambols'; a literary-register plural noun for sexual activity, most commonly in the phrase 'les ébats amoureux' (amorous frolics), used in press and literary prose as a polite circumlocution.","english_term":"amorous frolics","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Plural noun only; rarely used in singular. Appears frequently in French tabloid and broadsheet journalism when describing sexual activity caught on camera or disclosed in scandal."}
{"term":"consommer","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"consummation","concept":"consummation","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To consummate; primarily used in the phrase 'consommer le mariage' for the first act of marital intercourse; extended colloquially to any sex act between new partners.","english_term":"to consummate","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Also used humorously in modern dating contexts ('on a consumé' = 'we did it'). The legal-religious framing around marriage consummation is historically salient in French civil and canon law."}
{"term":"fréquenter","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_relationship","concept":"sexual_relationship","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To date or regularly associate with someone in a romantic or sexual capacity; 'il la fréquente' implies he is seeing her in an intimate sense.","english_term":"to see someone","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"This sexual/romantic sense is now somewhat dated; younger speakers more commonly use 'sortir avec'. The sexual inference is heavily context-dependent and may imply an unofficial or clandestine relationship."}
{"term":"connaître bibliquement","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To know someone biblically; a humorous or ironic allusion to the Old Testament use of 'to know' (Hebrew yada) as a euphemism for sexual intercourse.","english_term":"to know someone in the biblical sense","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used self-consciously as a winking circumlocution rather than a sincere euphemism; awareness of the biblical source text is part of the register. Essentially identical in function to the English calque."}
{"term":"se donner","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To give oneself sexually; to surrender to a lover; carries connotations of emotional yielding and is often applied to a first sexual experience or an act of romantic capitulation.","english_term":"to give oneself (to someone)","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Implies the subject is the receptive or yielding partner; more commonly applied to women in traditional literary usage. The reflexive construction signals self-direction, framing the act as a gift given."}
{"term":"s'offrir","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To offer oneself sexually; to make oneself available for sex; can be used both reflexively (elle s'est offerte à lui = she gave herself to him) and transitively (s'offrir quelqu'un = to treat oneself to someone sexually).","english_term":"to offer oneself","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The transitive construction 's'offrir quelqu'un' carries a notably acquisitive or indulgent flavor. Depending on context, can shade toward suggesting sex work or deliberate seduction."}
{"term":"la petite mort","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"orgasm","concept":"orgasm","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'The little death'; a French literary and poetic term for orgasm, evoking the momentary ego dissolution, loss of consciousness, or metaphorical death-like surrender experienced at climax.","english_term":"the little death","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Internationally recognized through French cultural export and has been adopted as a loanword in English literary and academic discourse. In French, the register is firmly literary rather than clinical or colloquial."}
{"term":"plaisir charnel","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"carnal_desire","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Carnal pleasure'; a literary and theological phrase denoting bodily, especially sexual, pleasure as distinct from spiritual or intellectual satisfaction.","english_term":"carnal pleasure","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Appears frequently in religious texts contrasting the pleasures of the flesh with spiritual virtue; not used in everyday speech. The adjective 'charnel' retains a solemn, Augustinian coloring."}
{"term":"les jeux de l'amour","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_activity_general","concept":"sexual_activity_general","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'The games of love'; a literary euphemism for sexual foreplay and intercourse, treating lovemaking as a playful erotic game between partners.","english_term":"the games of love","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Associated with 17th–19th century French erotic and romantic literature; also the title of several classic French films. Now feels deliberately literary or affected when used."}
{"term":"partager le lit","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_implied","concept":"intercourse_implied","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'To share the bed'; a common French euphemism for sleeping with someone, implying an ongoing sexual or intimate relationship rather than a single encounter.","english_term":"to share someone's bed","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Can refer non-sexually to children or travelers sharing a bed, so sexual interpretation is context-dependent; typically disambiguated by pronoun and relational context."}
{"term":"s'abandonner","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"sexual_activity_general","concept":"sexual_activity_general","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To abandon oneself; to surrender to passion or desire, releasing restraint and giving oneself over entirely to erotic pleasure.","english_term":"to abandon oneself (to pleasure)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Typically requires a prepositional complement ('s'abandonner au plaisir', 's'abandonner à ses désirs'); the sexual meaning is contextual rather than inherent to the verb."}
{"term":"avoir des rapports","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":5,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":94,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'To have intercourse'; the most clinically neutral and broadly used French euphemism for sexual intercourse, standard in medical, legal, educational, and journalistic contexts as 'avoir des rapports sexuels'.","english_term":"to have intercourse","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The most universally understood French phrase for sex across all registers. The adjective expansion 'sexuels' removes ambiguity completely; without it, the phrase can refer to any kind of relations."}
{"term":"avoir (quelqu'un)","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_conquest","concept":"intercourse_conquest","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'To have (someone)'; colloquial slang for having sex with someone, framed as conquest or successful seduction; 'il l'a eu' = 'he got her'.","english_term":"to have (someone)","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Also means 'to fool or trick someone' in French, so the sexual vs non-sexual reading requires context. The acquisitive framing implies the speaker was the active or pursuing partner."}
{"term":"en avoir","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_opportunistic","concept":"intercourse_opportunistic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":25,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'To get some'; colloquial slang for having sex, framing the act as obtaining something desirable; the sexual sense is highly context-dependent.","english_term":"to get some","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The same phrase 'en avoir' more commonly means 'to have guts/courage' (en avoir dans le pantalon), so the sexual reading is only one disambiguation; neither sense is inherently more primary."}
{"term":"faire la bête à deux dos","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"euphemisms_idioms","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'To make the beast with two backs'; a Rabelaisian idiom for sexual intercourse, evoking the image of two bodies joined together forming a single two-backed creature.","english_term":"to make the beast with two backs","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Originates in Rabelais' Gargantua (1534); Shakespeare borrowed it in Othello (1603). Today the phrase reads as deliberately archaic, literary, or humorously affected. Recognized by educated speakers."}
{"term":"fornication","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual intercourse between unmarried persons; a formal ecclesiastical and legal term carrying inherent moral condemnation, drawn from Latin fornicatio.","english_term":"fornication","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Cognate with English 'fornication'; used primarily in theological, canonical law, and formal literary registers. Rarely used in everyday French speech outside ironic or solemn religious contexts."}
{"term":"paillardise","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":32,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Bawdiness' or 'ribaldry'; an archaic French noun for sexual licentiousness, lewd behavior, or the disposition toward crude sexual humor, strongly associated with the fabliaux tradition and Rabelais.","english_term":"bawdiness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derived from 'paillard' (lecher, debaucher, from paille = straw, referencing rolling in the hay). Encountered almost exclusively in historical, literary-critical, or medieval studies contexts today."}
{"term":"bougrerie","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sodomy","concept":"sodomy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":65,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":50,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An archaic and historically highly offensive French term for sodomy, particularly anal intercourse between men; derived from 'bougre' (bugger), itself from the Bogomil heretics of Bulgaria suspected of heterodox sexual practices.","english_term":"buggery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Etymologically parallel to English 'buggery'; used in Old Regime French law to criminalize anal intercourse. Now effectively obsolete outside historical texts. The attenuated descendant 'bougre' survives as a mild generalized expletive or term of address."}
{"term":"coïtion","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":5,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Coition'; a clinical and classical Latin-derived noun for sexual intercourse, synonymous with 'coït'; now largely displaced by 'coït' or 'rapport sexuel' in modern medical usage.","english_term":"coition","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Technically a Latinate noun form parallel to 'coït' (coitus); appears in 18th–19th century medical, scientific, and philosophical texts. Recognition today limited to educated speakers with medical or classical training."}
{"term":"luxure","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Lust'; one of the seven deadly sins (les sept péchés capitaux) in Catholic moral theology, denoting excessive carnal desire and sexual immoderation as a spiritual failing.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Critical false cognate: French 'luxure' means lust/lechery, not luxury (which is 'luxe' in French). Recognized by most French speakers through religious and literary education; actively used in theological and literary registers."}
{"term":"débauche","lang":"fr","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":30,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Debauchery'; excessive indulgence in sexual pleasures and moral dissipation; a formal and literary term for licentiousness combined with drinking and sensual overindulgence.","english_term":"debauchery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More alive in contemporary French than its English cognate; 'une nuit de débauche' or 'se livrer à la débauche' appear regularly in journalistic and literary prose. Also gives the verb 'débaucher' (to lead astray, or colloquially to poach an employee)."}
